\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\title{Contrastive Ranking for Feed Recommendation}
\author{Anonymous}
\date{May 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Feed ranking quality depends on how well the model separates the items a user will engage with from the rest of the candidate pool. We study a contrastive dual-tower ranker trained on in-batch negatives and report the configuration that proved stable across three weeks of logged traffic.

\section{Model}
Each user-item pair is encoded by a dual-tower network, and the cosine similarity of the two tower outputs is scaled by a temperature coefficient $\tau = 0.7$ before the softmax over in-batch negatives. A dropout rate of 0.15 is applied to both tower outputs during training to reduce co-adaptation.

\section{Training}
We optimize the model with AdamW, using a learning rate of 0.0003 and a weight decay of 0.01 applied to all dense layers. Training runs for three epochs over the logged impression data with early stopping on the validation split.

\section{Serving}
At serving time, only items whose retrieval confidence exceeds 0.9 enter the re-ranking stage; the remaining candidates are discarded before the final ordering is produced.

\section{Conclusion}
The contrastive ranker with the configuration above met the offline quality bar and has been running in the shadow deployment without incidents.
\end{document}
